{
 "id": "2026-li-hospital",
 "name": "Hospital Landscapes - Visual Comfort Rating",
 "description": "Faithful replication with the original photographs: 30 photographs of outdoor landscapes at three Chinese hospitals (Jinan, Hangzhou and Sanya), taken along the hospitals' paths at 1.70 m eye height and chosen as three representative images for each of ten landscape character groups, from broad views of green vegetation to enclosed views between buildings and views of vegetation with water. Participants answer the paper's eight background questions with its answer categories (gender, age group, education, occupation, urban or rural living area, whether they have ever visited a hospital, whether they have stayed in a hospital for more than 24 h, and whether they have a background in healthcare), then see the 30 photographs one at a time in random order with no two images of the same group in a row, as in the paper, and answer its question Does this scene enhance visual comfort and therapeutic relaxation within the hospital setting? on a 5-point Likert scale from 1 = strongly disagree to 5 = strongly agree. Rebuilt from Li, Yue, Mundher & Abdulghani (2026), Data-efficient image transformer for landscape character classification and visual comfort prediction in Chinese hospitals, Frontiers in Artificial Intelligence 9: 1733709, with the photographs from its Supplementary Table A and the question from Section 2.3 (CC BY 4.0). Image ids: A01-J03 as coded in Supplementary Table A (letter = landscape character group); answers are in comfort, keyed by image id; the group of each image is stored in the page's original-study block. Analysis as in the paper: mean visual comfort per image and per landscape character group (mean of its three images); the paper then used the group means as labels to train a vision transformer on 488 hospital images. The paper (252 online respondents) found every group above 3, the lowest scores for enclosed views between buildings (group E, 3.13) and views with a building behind the vegetation (group F, 3.25), and the highest for broad views of vegetation with a building (group C, 3.62) and vegetation with water (group J, 3.63). Differences from the original study: (1) the first five background questions are worded by us with the paper's answer categories from its Table 3; the last three use the paper's wording; (2) the paper recruited 252 members of the general public on an online survey platform it does not name; here anyone can take part; (3) the photographs are the supplement copies (about 500 x 280 px).",
